From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: PS5: boolean socket options read back as 1.

getsockopt of a boolean option gave a Windows program what the console's FreeBSD kernel answered: the option's flag
bit, SO_BROADCAST as 0x20 and TCP_NODELAY as TF_NODELAY's bit, where Windows and Linux answer 1 (ws2_32's sock test:
sock.c:1413, 1424, 1435, 1668 and 1712 on the console in both builds, 2026-10-25). A program that compares the value
with TRUE (a socket library that checks its own setting) took a set option for unset. The boolean SOL_SOCKET options
and TCP_NODELAY are now 0 or 1, in an int or a one-byte buffer.
---
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/socket.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/socket.c
@@ -1587,6 +1587,18 @@
     ret = getsockopt( fd, level, option, out_buffer, &len );
     if (needs_close) close( fd );
     if (ret) return sock_errno_to_status( errno );
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* The console's FreeBSD kernel reads a boolean option back as its flag bit (SO_BROADCAST as 0x20, TCP_NODELAY as
+     * TF_NODELAY's), where Windows and Linux give 1 (ws2_32 sock.c:1413-1435, 1668, 1712, 2026-10-25). */
+    if (len && ((level == SOL_SOCKET && (option == SO_DEBUG || option == SO_ACCEPTCONN || option == SO_REUSEADDR ||
+                                         option == SO_KEEPALIVE || option == SO_DONTROUTE || option == SO_BROADCAST ||
+                                         option == SO_OOBINLINE)) ||
+                (level == IPPROTO_TCP && option == TCP_NODELAY)))
+    {
+        if (len >= sizeof(int)) *(int *)out_buffer = !!*(int *)out_buffer;
+        else *(char *)out_buffer = !!*(char *)out_buffer;
+    }
+#endif
     if (io)
     {
         io->Status = STATUS_SUCCESS;
